 largest number that we  can put on the mystery die  so notice the only way we can get a sum  of twelve is six plus six  so that means we're going to need three  faces to be labeled with six that's the  only way we can get three sums of twelve  now by similar logic how can we get a  sum of one  well zero is the smallest number for the  mystery die and the only way we can get  a sum of one is if we do zero plus one  so we're going to need three faces to be  labeled as zero  so now we've actually labeled all six  faces  do we have a solution  let's fill in the rest of the sums  the first row goes from one to six and  the next two rows will be the same  then this row will go from seven to  twelve and the next two rows will be the  same  and look at this table carefully  each sum from one to twelve appears  exactly three times and by magic we have  found the answer  the mystery die needs to be labeled with  three faces of zero and three faces of  six  so we kind of lucked into the answer  and that's good for this problem and it  would be great in an interview  but  how would you solve this problem in a  more systematic way  i want to show you another method it'll  be a lot more complicated but it's a  powerful tool to solve such problems  the method is called probability  generating functions  if z is a discrete random variable on  the non-negative integers and p i is the  probability that z is equal to i  then we define the probability  generating function of z as follows  it'll be the polynomial with terms p sub  i multiplied by x to the power of i  so let's create the probability  generating function for the fair die  well the fair die takes the realizations  of one through six  and each is equally likely to occur with  a chance of 1 over 6.  so what's its probability generating  function  what we do is we attach the coefficients  of x of i to the probability of i  we can now factor out one over six  so here's the probability generating  function of the fair die  we 